Verify this factual, general-knowledge claim using web search.

Claim: "{{ claim_text }}"

## Critical instruction

Search for real, current sources BEFORE deciding. Do not answer from memory alone,
even if the claim seems obviously true or false to you — models can be confidently
wrong about technical facts (for example, mistakenly believing a well-known
declarative/logic language is "low-level" when it is not). Your own training-time
belief is not evidence; a retrieved source is.

## Evidence hierarchy (prefer higher tiers when sources disagree)

1. Official documentation (the language/framework/database's own docs)
2. Academic or authoritative reference sources
3. Known trusted technical sources
4. General web sources (lowest trust — lean toward "unclear" rather than a
   confident verdict if this is the only tier available)

## Output

Return ONLY a JSON object. Do not use Markdown fences, prose before or after the
object, nested objects, or extra keys. The object must contain exactly these keys:
{
"status": "supported" | "contradicted" | "unclear",
"confidence": 0.0-1.0,
"reason": "one or two sentences, referencing what the retrieved source(s) actually say"
}

Rules:
- Use "contradicted" only when a strong-tier source (1 or 2 above) clearly conflicts
  with the claim AND you are highly confident (this will be enforced as confidence
  >= 0.75 by the caller regardless of what you report).
- If you cannot find a reliable source, or found only weak general-web sources,
  return status="unclear" — do NOT guess just to give a definitive-sounding answer.
